<!--
    (kuna) `option endptrbound` (P6 stack-frame-layout, default ON).  GH-468
    finding 7: a pointer-walk loop's one-past-the-end bound is not derivable
    from the C.

    f, at 0x140001000, is the MSVC x64 shape from the report: an 8-byte stack
    buffer built from two dword constants, walked bytewise, each byte handed to
    a call next to a std::string-like object that lives right after the buffer:

        140001021  mov  dword ptr [rbp+0x27],0x3e3c2f3e
        140001028  mov  dword ptr [rbp+0x2b],0x2e383036
        14000102f  lea  rsi,[rbp+0x27]          ; p = buf
        140001033  movzx edx,byte ptr [rsi]      ; *p
        140001036  lea  rcx,[rbp+0x2f]          ; &s
        14000103a  call push_back
        14000103f  inc  rsi
        140001042  lea  rax,[rbp+0x2f]          ; buf + 8
        140001046  cmp  rsi,rax
        140001049  jne  140001033

    [rbp+0x2f] is both one past the buffer and the address of s, so the frame
    model binds the bound to s: the loop renders `while (v3 != v2)`, the
    walking pointer takes s's element type, and the buffer becomes an
    `unsigned int` written through `_4_4_`.  With the option on, the walk
    (stack start, constant step, dereferenced with the step's width, compared
    against a stack address a whole number of steps later) makes [rbp+0x27,
    rbp+0x2f) one `char [8]`, and the bound is rebuilt on it as `&v1[8]`.

    k, at 0x140001080, computes the end address once into RDI and uses the
    same register as the call's `&s`.  The bound still renders on the buffer,
    but the call argument, which really is s, keeps rendering as s.

    g, at 0x140001100, is the NEGATIVE control: the walk has the same shape but
    an 8-byte store at [rbp+0x2b] straddles the walk's end, so no buffer can
    span the walk and g renders identically in both passes.

    P0 assertion: `option endptrbound off|on`.  Pass 1 (off) pins the bug
    (#1, #2), pass 2 (on) pins the fix (#3, #4, #5), and #6 holds in both.
-->
